# Conductor: critic on PR 2732 — voiding the Stripe hold on a terminal refusal — 2026-08-30
#
# DISPATCH: ./scripts/dispatch.sh git@github.com:Arlencho/olympus-platform.git wave-plans/conductor/2026-08-30-2732-void-hold-critic.plan --auto --retries 2
#
# Money path, human-authored by the VP Eng, open since 2026-08-24. This is the fix for the
# defect filed as #2715: the code keeps a Stripe authorisation alive to fund a retry that
# the client never offers, so a traveller can hold a live hold on a booking that failed.
# Standing rule: critics on all payment and state-machine PRs.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of PR 2732, titled fix(api) void the Stripe hold on a refusal the client calls terminal. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. The harness pushes the branch it checks out, so any commit on this producer ref is a production incident. Permitted writes are a /tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved, report BLOCKED with both SHAs. This PR is authored by a human colleague, so review the code and never the person, and do not comment on commit trailers or authorship. BACKGROUND, established by earlier reviews and filed as issue 2715: isRecoverableUserError in apps/api/internal/service/trip_booking.go classifies purely by HTTP status, so every Duffel 422 counts as user-recoverable and the Stripe authorisation is deliberately kept. The stated justification, restated as contract in api.yaml around lines 3754 to 3755, is that the hold is kept so the corrected retry still has a live intent. The defect is that on some arms the client never offers that retry: checkout-failure.ts maps a confirm-time 409 and 410 to OFFER_GONE with retryable false and routeOut true, and the Search again handler is wired to onClose, so the modal simply closes. One PaymentIntent exists per booking row, so searching again mints a second hold while the first is orphaned until Stripes roughly seven day expiry, and the reaper worker is paused. THE QUESTIONS THAT MATTER. One: after this change, is there any remaining arm where the server keeps an authorisation while the client treats the outcome as terminal. Enumerate the client mappings in apps/web/lib/checkout-failure.ts against the server statuses that keep a hold, and name any pair that still disagrees, rather than checking only the arm the PR names. Two: the inverse risk, which is worse. Does the change ever void a hold that a genuine retry still needs. Walk the paths where the traveller does get a working retry, for example a corrected passenger field on a fielded 422, and confirm the intent survives there. A voided hold on a recoverable error means the traveller cannot complete a booking they were about to pay for. Three: double-void and idempotency. If the void is attempted twice, or races the Stripe webhook, or the booking was already captured, what happens. Confirm a capture can never be turned into a void, and that a failed void does not abort the request or mask the original refusal. Four: confirm the api.yaml prose that asserts the retained-authorisation invariant is either updated in this PR or explicitly flagged as stale, since shipping the behaviour change while the contract still promises the old behaviour is the drift that produced this defect. Five: tests. Confirm a test proves the hold is actually voided on the terminal arm, that it fails without the change, and that no test was written to pass trivially. A diff touching no money-path file can still change money behaviour, so follow behaviour out to the client rather than reading the file list. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC PR 2732, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE, per-finding severity and file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | fix/2715-void-hold-on-client-terminal-409
